    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A concrete driveway is one of the biggest investments you can make in the outside of your home. Concrete is known for being hard-wearing, but it is not a "set it and forget it" surface. Summer heat, heavy rain and, above all, the freeze-thaw cycles of winter all put the slab under strain year after year.</p>
<p>Regular care is the key to keeping both the looks and the structure of your driveway in good shape. A simple seasonal routine stops small problems, such as hairline cracks, from growing into repairs that mean breaking out and relaying whole sections. This guide sets out what to do in each season to keep your concrete in good condition. How long a driveway lasts depends on how it was installed, how it is used, your climate and how well it is maintained, so the routine below is worth the effort.</p>

<h2 id="deep-cleaning">1. Spring: deep cleaning</h2>
<p>As the frosts end and the ground dries out, spring is the ideal time to assess what winter has left behind. Start with a thorough clean. Over the colder months, road salt, grit and grime from tyres build up on the surface, and they are more than unsightly: salt and chemical residues can attack the concrete if they are left to sit.</p>
<p>A pressure washer is the quickest way to lift embedded dirt, but use it with care. Choose a low setting and a fan nozzle, keep the wand moving and follow any guidance from your installer or sealer manufacturer, as too much pressure can etch or scar the surface. For mildew, moss or algae in damp, shaded corners, apply a biodegradable detergent or a cleaner made for concrete before rinsing, following the label and keeping run-off out of drains, ponds and watercourses. Clearing these deposits in spring stops them baking into the surface over the summer.</p>

<!--figure:pressure-washing-paving|Use a pressure washer on a low setting with a fan nozzle, kept moving and well back from the surface.-->

<h2 id="surface-inspection">2. Spring: surface inspection</h2>
<p>Once the driveway is clean and dry, walk its full length and look closely. Spring is when frost heave, the movement of soil as it freezes and thaws, shows its effects. Look for new cracks, old cracks that have widened, sections that have lifted or dropped, and spalling, where the top layer of concrete starts to flake or peel away.</p>
<p>Pay close attention to the joints, the planned grooves and gaps that let the slab move. If the flexible filler in them has dried out, cracked or come loose, it needs replacing. Spotting trouble early is your best defence: a small crack sealed in spring is a quick job, whereas one left open through a wet spring and summer lets water reach the base underneath and wash it away, which can lead to sinking.</p>

<h2 id="crack-repair">3. Summer: crack repair</h2>
<p>Summer is the season for repairs. Warm, dry weather gives fillers and sealants the best conditions to cure. Any crack wider than a hairline, roughly 3 mm (1/8 in) or more, is worth filling.</p>
<p>Clean the crack out first with a wire brush to remove loose material and dust. For narrow cracks, a liquid concrete crack filler or a flexible polyurethane sealant works well; because these products stay flexible, the repair can move with the slab as it expands in the heat instead of popping out. For wider gaps, a foam backer rod pushed into the crack before sealing gives the sealant a firm base and keeps it at the right depth. Follow the product instructions on application and drying times. Filling cracks in summer keeps rainwater out of the slab and stops weeds rooting in the gaps.</p>
<p>Wide cracks, cracks where one side sits higher than the other, or areas that have clearly sunk are signs of a problem in the base rather than the surface. Ask a driveway contractor to look at those before you fill them.</p>

<!--figure:crack-in-concrete-surface|Fill hairline cracks in summer, before winter frost can widen them.-->

<h2 id="stain-removal">4. Summer: stain removal</h2>
<p>Summer usually brings more activity on the driveway, from washing or working on the car to barbecues and parties, so oil and grease stains are more likely. Concrete is porous, so oil soaks into the surface and becomes much harder to shift the longer it is left.</p>
<p>Treat spills straight away. For fresh oil, cover the spot with cat litter or sawdust to soak up as much as possible, then sweep it up. For older stains, use a concrete degreaser or a paste of bicarbonate of soda and water, scrub firmly with a stiff brush and rinse. Follow the label on any cleaner and keep oily run-off away from drains. Dealing with stains promptly in warm weather stops the heat helping the oil work its way deeper into the concrete.</p>

<h2 id="debris-clearance">5. Autumn: clearing leaves and debris</h2>
<p>In autumn, fallen leaves become the main threat. A scattering looks pretty, but leaves left lying on concrete for weeks can cause problems. Wet leaves release tannins, the same natural compounds once used to tan leather, which can leave dark brown marks on pale concrete.</p>
<p>Piles of wet leaves also hold moisture against the surface, which can wear away any sealer faster. Make it a weekly habit while leaves are falling to sweep or blow leaves and pine needles off the driveway. This is also a good moment to check that gutters and downpipes are not emptying straight onto the edge of the slab, as repeated soaking can wash away the supporting ground and lead to cracks along the edges.</p>

<h2 id="protective-sealing">6. Autumn: protective sealing</h2>
<p>If your driveway has not been sealed for a few years, or water no longer beads on the surface, early autumn is a good time to reseal. A penetrating sealer, such as a silane or siloxane product, soaks into the concrete and helps it shed water, oil and salt.</p>
<p>Sealing before the first frosts matters because water that gets into the pores of unsealed concrete expands when it freezes, putting pressure on the surface and causing it to pop and scale. A sound coat of sealer greatly reduces how much water gets in. Apply it within the temperature range on the product label, only when the concrete is completely dry and no rain is forecast for the drying time the manufacturer gives, and check the sealer suits your type of concrete and finish.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> To check whether your sealer is still working, sprinkle a little water on the driveway on a dry day. If it beads up, the sealer is doing its job; if it darkens the concrete and soaks straight in, it is time to reseal.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="snow-management">7. Winter: clearing snow</h2>
<p>In winter, the way you clear snow has a big effect on how well your driveway survives until spring. The most important rule is to avoid metal-bladed shovels and snow blowers with metal-edged blades, which can scrape and chip the surface, especially where the slab is slightly uneven.</p>
<p>Use plastic shovels or tools with a rubber edge instead, and try to clear snow soon after it falls. Snow that is left to sit often melts a little in the daytime and refreezes as ice bonded to the concrete, which is much harder to remove without damaging the surface. If you pay someone to clear snow with a plough, ask them to fit protective shoes or a rubber edge to the blade so the steel does not scrape the slab.</p>

<!--figure:snow-blower-clearing-driveway|Clear snow promptly with a plastic shovel or snow blower rather than relying on salt.-->

<h2 id="salt-alternatives">8. Winter: alternatives to rock salt</h2>
<p>Rock salt (sodium chloride) is one of the worst things you can put on concrete. It melts ice effectively, but meltwater soaks into the surface and can refreeze, adding to freeze-thaw damage, and salt can corrode any steel reinforcement inside the slab. Avoid rock salt altogether on new concrete and close to metal such as drainage channels or gates.</p>
<p>For grip, a thin layer of sand gives traction without the chemical effects; sweep it up in spring so it does not wash into drains. If you need a de-icer, choose a product your installer or sealer manufacturer approves; calcium magnesium acetate (CMA) is often described as gentler on concrete. Avoid de-icers containing ammonium nitrate or ammonium sulphate, which can attack concrete. Always follow the product label.</p>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>How often should I seal my concrete driveway?</h3>
<p>Every few years, or as often as the sealer label advises. Driveways in harsh winters or with heavy traffic may need it more often. The simplest test is to watch the surface when it rains: if water stops beading, it is time for a fresh coat.</p>
<h3>Can I use a pressure washer on new concrete?</h3>
<p>Not straight away. New concrete keeps gaining strength for some time after it is laid, and high pressure can damage the fine surface layer. Ask your contractor when it is safe; until then, use a garden hose and a soft broom.</p>
<h3>Why is my concrete flaking off in small pieces?</h3>
<p>This is called spalling or scaling. It is usually caused by freeze-thaw damage or de-icing salts. Once it starts it can be hard to stop, so clean the area, ask a contractor whether a concrete resurfacer is suitable, and seal the driveway well afterwards.</p>
<h3>Are all cracks a sign of a failing driveway?</h3>
<p>No. Concrete shrinks slightly as it hardens, which often causes fine shrinkage cracks. As long as cracks stay narrow and both sides are level with each other, they are usually cosmetic and easy to fill. Widening cracks, or cracks with one side higher than the other, are worth having checked.</p>
<h3>Is it okay to park heavy vehicles on my driveway?</h3>
<p>That depends on how your driveway was built. Residential slabs are commonly around 100 mm (4 in) thick for cars; heavy delivery lorries, skips, motorhomes or building machinery can exceed what the slab was designed for and cause cracking or sinking. Ask your contractor what your driveway can take, and keep heavy loads away from the edges, where support is weakest.</p>
<h3>Do I need permission to replace or extend my driveway?</h3>
<p>Possibly. In England, a new or replacement hard surface of more than 5 m² in a front garden generally needs planning permission unless it is permeable or drains to a permeable area such as a lawn or border, and a new dropped kerb needs the council's permission. In the US, check local permits and any HOA rules. Either way, check with your local authority before you start.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>A little attention each season, cleaning and checking in spring, repairing in summer, clearing and sealing in autumn and treating the surface gently in winter, keeps a concrete driveway looking smart and protects the slab underneath. If you are planning a new driveway, read about the <a href="/blog/concrete-driveway-mistakes">concrete driveway mistakes</a> that are worth avoiding, or compare the <a href="/blog/paver-driveway-pros-and-cons">pros and cons of a paver driveway</a>. For help pulling the front garden and outdoor spaces together, explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service, or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a> with a designer.</p>
